"The distinction changes the entire response. Qi deficiency calls for rest and gentle replenishment — pushing a depleted system harder only depletes it further. Qi stagnation calls for movement and release — resting a stuck system only lets it stay stuck longer. Mistaking one for the other, by resting when movement was needed or pushing through activity when depletion was the real issue, is a common and unhelpful error, which is exactly why telling them apart is treated as a first, essential step rather than an afterthought.": "c673697bbadc",
"The road was blocked by a fallen tree.": "b7d1bdbe95c4",
"The two can produce a surprisingly similar complaint — a person might describe both as \"tired\" or \"not right\" — but the details point in different directions. Qi deficiency tends to produce a dull, constant fatigue that worsens with exertion and improves with rest; the person often speaks softly and prefers to sit still. Qi stagnation tends to produce a distending, moving discomfort — often in the chest or abdomen — that comes and goes with mood, worsens with sitting still, and can actually improve somewhat with movement or a long sigh, since sighing is traditionally read as the body's own attempt to force stuck qi to move.": "280c75116633",
"This course's first unit described qi deficiency in detail: a general shortage, where the body's functions aren't being carried out with full force. Chinese medicine also describes a second, easily confused problem with the opposite mechanism: qi stagnation, where the overall amount of qi may be perfectly adequate, but its movement has become blocked in a specific place, the way a traffic jam isn't a shortage of cars but a failure of cars to keep moving.": "0758cb16cc72",
